Looking at the diff: it modifies `YAML_FLOAT_EXPLICIT_PATTERN` in the JSON-schema float tag, changing `[-+]?[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]*)?(?:[eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?` to `[-+]?[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]*)(?:[eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?` — dropping the `?` that made the decimal point optional in the first alternative.

This pattern is only consulted in `resolveYamlFloat$1` when `isExplicit === true`, i.e. when a scalar carries an explicit tag like `!!float` under `JSON_SCHEMA`. Consequences on the patched build:

- `load('!!float 123', { schema: JSON_SCHEMA })` — previously matched the pattern and returned `123`; now the pattern no longer matches, so `NOT_RESOLVED` is returned and the constructor throws `cannot resolve a node with !<tag:yaml.org,2002:float> explicit tag`.
- Same for `!!float 1e5` and `!!float -1` (exponent-only / digits-only forms). Dotted forms (`123.`, `123.45`, `.inf`, `.nan`) and all implicit resolution (which uses the unchanged `YAML_FLOAT_IMPLICIT_PATTERN`) are unaffected.

The YAML 1.2 JSON-schema float regex explicitly permits digits without a fractional part (`[0-9]+(\.[0-9]*)?`), and the core float tag's explicit pattern (used by the default `CORE_SCHEMA`) retains the optional dot — so this is a regression in explicit-tag resolution, even though the existing suite doesn't exercise `!!float <digits>` under `JSON_SCHEMA`.

